<h1>
A Global Pathway to Net Zero Buildings:
A Correlation Network Analysis of Building Information Modelling (BIM)-based Whole Life Carbon Assessment Challenges
</h1>

<p class="lead">
<h2>Why we need correlation network analysis for BIM-based whole life carbon assessment?</h2>
BIM-based whole life carbon assessment drives global digitalisation strategies to achieve net zero targets in buildings.
However, existing studies focus excessively on its technical aspects (e.g., BIM plug-ins and
WLCA automation); hence, overlooking socio-economic, institutional, political, and cultural factors.

<strong> Rather than asking "which factor matters most?", we ask "how do factors interact and shape decisions?"</strong>
</p>

<p>
Sustainable construction studies commonly rely on the below research methodologies with several limitations (see Figure 1)
</p>

<ul>
<li>Regression analysis: assume linear relationships between independent and dependent variables for predictions</li>

<li>Structural Equation Modeling (SEM): assume a predefined causal relationship</li>

<li>Principal Component Analysis (PCA): reduce multiple variables into factors but can hide network positions</li>

<li>Social Network Analysis (SNA): focuses on stakeholders and organisations but not the variables themselves</li>
</ul>

