<img
src="/research/global-pathway/4-CorHeatMap-All.png"
class="project-figure"
/>
<figcaption>
Figure 4. Correlation heatmap for all regions
</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>
Regarding the network graphs, as seen from Figure 5, a larger and darker node signifies a stronger variable that exerts greater systemic
influence over other nodes, while a smaller and lighter node suggests a weaker variable that is
highly dependent on other nodes in the network. The size of the node is based on its respective
degree centrality value, which measures the number of connections that a node form with other
nodes in the same graph. 
</p>

<p>
For example, the largest node is #Challenge 20 “lack of sitespecific information to render accurate WLCA calculations”
with a darker green colour. 
#Challenge 20 has branched out the largest number of edges, forming connections with 13 other challenges,
establishing it as the core systemic challenge for BIM-based whole life carbon assessment globally.

A caveat is that the central role of #Challenge 20 is built on undirected correlation coefficients and thus does not stimulate
other challenges’ occurrence. We can only imply that the highest number of relationships stems
from the challenge #Challenge 20, but no causality is drawn among the challenges with #Challenge 20.

</p>

